SUMMARY
Something’s not right. Suspicious traffic has been spotted, and early signs point to something deeper.

LEARNING OBJECTIVES
-  Use Azure Data Explorer and Kusto Query Language (KQL) to explore security data across Convoy Street Interactive.
- Gather indicators of compromise (IOCs) from suspicious activity tied to player-facing and internal systems.
- Enrich findings with threat intelligence to build context around attacker infrastructure and potential attribution.
- Apply your understanding of adversary behavior to guide and refine the hunt.
- Investigate attacker movement across systems, including lateral movement and persistence.
- Practice the hunt/intel feedback loop to piece together the intrusion and uncover what the attacker was really after.

SOURCE
https://kc7cyber.com/challenges/1100

STEPS TAKEN
Section 1: Detection to Hypothesis
Do we have any records of traffic coming from 185.210.94.2? If so, how many?
InboundNetworkEvents
| where src_ip == "185.210.94.2"
Answer: 24

Do we have any records of a user successfully logging in from 185.210.94.2? If so, how many?
AuthenticationEvents
| where src_ip == "185.210.94.2"
Answer: no

What domain does 185.210.94.2 resolve to?
PassiveDns
| where ip == "185.210.94.2"
Answer: telemetryfinancegaming.net

How many distinct IPs are associated with telemetryfinancegaming.net in the PassiveDns table?
PassiveDns
| where domain == "telemetryfinancegaming.net"
Answer: 3

How many additional domains do we identify from this pivot?
let ips=
PassiveDns
| where domain == "telemetryfinancegaming.net"
| project ip;
PassiveDns
| where ip in (ips)
| distinct domain
Answer: 4

Which TLD gives us our first big clue about what country the actor behind these IPs might be operating out of?
Answer: cn


Section 2: Context is Everything
How many employees received emails from those suspicious domains?
Email
| where links has_any ("telemetry-finance-gaming.net", "gaming-telemetry-finance.cn", "telemetryfinancegaming.net", "gaming-telemetry-finance.com", "gamingtelemetryfinance.cn")
Answer: 4

What is the name of the file attached to the email sent to the first employee observed there?
Answer: GreasyTender_Audit_Report.pdf

How many different filenames are attached to emails from those suspicious domains?
Answer: 2

In total, how many employees received an email with one of those files attached?
Email
| where attachments has_any ("GreasyTender_Audit_Report.pdf", "GT_TelemetrySync.log.pdf")
| distinct recipient
Answer: 10

What is the timestamp for the first email sent by this actor?
Answer: 2024-03-08T16:39:47.000Z

What is the name of the first employee to receive an email from this actor?
Answer: Peter Orourke

How many distinct roles were targeted?
let recipients=
Email
| where attachments has_any ("GreasyTender_Audit_Report.pdf", "GT_TelemetrySync.log.pdf")
| distinct recipient;
Employees
| where email_addr in (recipients)
| distinct role
Answer: 3

How many employees had one of these attachments created on their machine?
FileCreationEvents
| where filename has_any ("GreasyTender_Audit_Report.pdf", "GT_TelemetrySync.log.pdf")
| distinct hostname
Answer: 10

What is the name of the file that was created immediately after the attachment was opened?
FileCreationEvents
| where hostname == "LFUZ-LAPTOP"
| where timestamp >= datetime(2024-03-09T09:12:47.000Z)
Answer: output_beacon_monitor.exe

What server does the host attempt to connect to?
ProcessEvents
| where hostname == "LFUZ-LAPTOP"
| where timestamp >= datetime(2024-03-09T09:12:47.000Z)
Answer: hustlefinance.cn.

How many file types is the attacker attempting to collect from the Greasy Tender directory?
Answer: 4

What is the name of the file that was created to store stolen Chrome credentials?
Answer: stolen_chrome_creds.db

What is the timestamp of the last log-clearing command observed on this host?
Answer: 2024-03-21T21:18:17.000Z


Section 3: Access to Impact
Enter the full command line the attacker used to establish persistence.
ProcessEvents
| where hostname == "GUBQ-LAPTOP"
| where timestamp >= datetime(2024-03-09T09:12:47.000Z)
Answer:  schtasks /create /tn \"TelemetrySync\" /tr \"powershell.exe -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File C:\\Windows\\System32\\telemetry_sync.ps1\" /sc onlogon /rl highest

How many machines did the attacker run this command on?
ProcessEvents
| where process_commandline contains "schtasks"
Answer: 3

How many total login events are associated with this set of compromised users?
let comp_hosts=
ProcessEvents
| where process_commandline contains "schtasks"
| distinct hostname;
let comp_users=
Employees
| where hostname in (comp_hosts)
| project username;
AuthenticationEvents
| where username in (comp_users)
Answer: 468

How many successful login events are associated with this set of compromised users?
let comp_hosts=
ProcessEvents
| where process_commandline contains "schtasks"
| distinct hostname;
let comp_users=
Employees
| where hostname in (comp_hosts)
| project username;
AuthenticationEvents
| where username in (comp_users)
| where result contains "Success"
Answer: 236

How many distinct hostnames were successfully logged into by this set of compromised users?
let comp_hosts=
ProcessEvents
| where process_commandline contains "schtasks"
| distinct hostname;
let comp_users=
Employees
| where hostname in (comp_hosts)
| project username;
AuthenticationEvents
| where username in (comp_users)
| where result contains "Success"
| distinct hostname
Answer: 6

Which hostname stands out as the likely target the attackers were most interested in compromising using the stolen credentials?
Answer: GreasyTenderVault

Which employee’s machine was used by the attacker to move laterally to that system?
let comp_hosts=
ProcessEvents
| where process_commandline contains "schtasks"
| distinct hostname;
let comp_users=
Employees
| where hostname in (comp_hosts)
